use std
into drone.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with dr_ is exported). It is the design engine of the Drone workspace in Amni-CAD: from a design brief it lays out the airframe (motor positions, arms, body plates, ducts or a saucer ring), places the flight stack, camera, video transmitter, receiver and battery so nothing touches, checks every pair of parts for exact overlap and clearance, grows the frame until the propellers clear everything, and estimates thrust, hover power and flight time. Lengths are millimeters, masses grams, angles radians unless a name says degrees. X points right, Y points forward (the nose), Z points up.

```amni
extern fn dr_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "dr_view_f64";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NA` (motor count), `NI` (placed items), `NC` (check records), `ON` (optimizer parameter count), `OD` (parameter being probed), `OS` (probe direction, 1 or minus 1), `OK` (scores told), `OB` (score budget), `OI` (1 once the current cycle improved), `OH` (1 once the starting point is scored). Reals, all starting at 0: `OV` (overlap volume of the last pair), `GP` (gap of the last pair), `OF` (best optimizer score), `FD` (drag force), `FL` (lift force), `FT` (thrust force). Growable f64 arrays: `DP` (64 brief values), `AR` (12 per motor), `BP` (12 body values), `IT` (16 per placed item), `CK` (8 per check record), `PF` (16 results), `HO` (3 per frame hole), `MV` (64 placement shifts, one per item slot), `AE` (3 per tilt row of the aero table), `TR` (8 per airspeed row of the trim table), `OX` (5 per optimizer parameter), `OC` (16 candidate values), `OM` (9 per scored candidate: its 8 parameter values, then its score).

`DP` holds the brief: 0 design type (0 X quad, 1 stretched X, 2 deadcat, 3 H, 4 hexacopter, 5 ducted cinewhoop, 6 toothpick, 7 long-range stretched X, 8 saucer with one rim-driven fan, 9 twin ring with two stacked rim-driven fans spinning opposite ways), 1 propeller diameter, 2 smallest gap between propeller tips, 3 smallest gap between a propeller and anything else, 4 bottom plate and arm thickness, 5 flight stack hole spacing, 6 motor hole spacing, 7 motor bell diameter, 8 motor height, 9 arm width at the motor, 10 battery length (along Y), 11 battery width (along X), 12 battery height, 13 camera size (a cube), 14 flight stack height, 15 transmitter width, 16 transmitter length, 17 transmitter height, 18 receiver width, 19 receiver length, 20 receiver height, 21 duct wall thickness, 22 duct, saucer ring or twin ring duct height, 23 top plate thickness, 24 blade count, 25 propeller seat height above the motor, 26 propeller hub height, 27 flight stack board size (square), 28 motor mass, 29 propeller mass, 30 battery mass, 31 flight stack mass, 32 transmitter mass, 33 receiver mass, 34 camera mass, 35 frame density in grams per cubic centimeter, 36 motor kv, 37 battery cells in series, 38 battery capacity in mAh, 39 propeller thrust coefficient, 40 propeller power coefficient, 41 largest current per motor in amps, 42 extra mass (straps, wires, screws), 43 arm sweep in degrees away from the forward axis (stretched X), 44 smallest standoff height, 45 battery cells in parallel, 46 battery mount (0 on the top plate, 1 under the bottom plate), 47 fan inner radius of types 8 and 9, 48 saucer hollow core radius, 49 smallest gap between two unrelated parts, 50 gap between a propeller tip and its duct, 51 fixed motor-to-motor diagonal wheelbase (0 lets the engine choose the smallest that clears), 52 nose fairing reach beyond the rim shell (types 8 and 9), 53 nose edge height as a fraction of the ring height, 54 nose shape exponent (2 round, toward 1 sharp).

`AR` holds one record of 12 per motor: 0 motor x, 1 motor y, 2 direction angle from the center to the motor, 3 arm root x, 4 arm root y, 5 arm width at the root, 6 arm width at the motor, 7 arm length, 8 duct inner radius (0 without a duct), 9 duct outer radius, 10 spin direction (1 or minus 1), 11 unused.

`BP` holds the body: 0 left x, 1 rear y, 2 right x, 3 front y of the body plate rectangle, 4 corner radius, 5 standoff height, 6 bottom plate thickness, 7 top plate thickness, 8 height of the bottom of the propeller hubs, 9 saucer fan inner radius, 10 distance from the center to each motor, 11 saucer duct wall inner radius.

`IT` holds one record of 16 per placed item: 0 kind (1 bottom plate, 2 top plate, 3 standoff, 4 motor, 5 propeller, 6 duct, 7 flight stack, 8 camera, 9 video transmitter, 10 receiver, 11 battery, 12 saucer or twin ring inner duct wall, 13 saucer spoke mount or twin ring hub band and arms, 14 saucer outer rim shell or twin ring chassis, 15 saucer skin or twin ring vanes and inlet guard), 1 shape (0 box, 1 upright cylinder, 2 upright ring), 2 center x, 3 center y, 4 base z, 5 box width or outer radius, 6 box depth or inner radius of a ring, 7 height, 8 mass, 9 parent item index (minus 1 for none), 10 motor index (minus 1 for none), 11 axis it may move along (0 fixed, 1 x, 2 y, 3 z), 12 direction it moves (1 or minus 1), 13 group (0 frame, 1 propulsion, 2 electronics, 3 power), 14 placement slot (its index into `MV`), 15 unused.

`CK` holds one record of 8 per problem: first item, second item, kind (1 the two overlap, 2 closer than allowed), overlap volume, gap, required gap, 0, 0.

`PF` holds the results: 0 all-up mass, 1 frame mass, 2 total full-throttle thrust in grams, 3 thrust-to-weight ratio, 4 hover throttle as a fraction of full speed, 5 hover current in amps, 6 hover power in watts, 7 flight time in minutes, 8 full-throttle current in amps, 9 diagonal wheelbase, 10 overall width, 11 overall length, 12 overall height, 13 number of problems, 14 disc loading in grams per square centimeter, 15 top speed in meters per second from the last `dr_trim`, 16 the margin under the weight limit in grams.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `rs(xs, n)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied and then gets `reserve`(n).

The exported function `dr_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes through dr_view_f64: 1 `DP`, 2 `AR`, 3 `BP`, 4 `IT`, 5 `CK`, 6 `PF`, 7 `HO`, 8 `AE`, 9 `TR`, 10 `OX`, 11 `OC`. It returns 0.

The exported function `dr_setup()` sizes `DP` to 64 zeros, `BP` to 12 zeros, `PF` to 24 zeros `MV` to 64 zeros, `AE` to 192 zeros, `TR` to 320 zeros, `OX` to 80 zeros, `OC` to 16 zeros and `OM` to 576 zeros with `zf`, gives `AR` `rs` of 12 times 12, `IT` `rs` of 16 times 96, `CK` `rs` of 8 times 1024 and `HO` `rs` of 3 times 160, calls `dr_views` and returns 0.

`dv(i, d)` returns `DP` entry i when it is above 0 and d otherwise.

`ig(i, f)` returns field f of item i, the `IT` entry at 16 times i plus f. `isx(i, f, v)` sets that entry to v.

Item bounds. `lo(i, a)` and `hi(i, a)` give the low and high extent of item i along axis a (0 x, 1 y, 2 z): along z they are the base z and the base z plus the height; along x and y a box spans its center minus and plus half its width (x) or depth (y), while a cylinder or ring spans its center minus and plus its outer radius.

`lens(d, r1, r2)` is the area shared by two circles of radii r1 and r2 whose centers are d apart, using the standard circle lens formula: 0 when either radius is at most 0 or d is at least r1 plus r2, pi times the smaller radius squared when d is at most the absolute difference of the radii, and otherwise the lens area.

`cbox(cx, cy, r, x0, y0, x1, y1)` is the area shared by the circle of radius r centered at (cx, cy) and the rectangle from (x0, y0) to (x1, y1). It returns 0 when r is at most 0 or the x spans do not overlap. Otherwise it splits the shared x span (the larger of cx minus r and x0 to the smaller of cx plus r and x1) into 128 equal slices; at each slice's middle x, the circle covers y within the square root of (r squared minus the squared x distance to cx) of cy, that range is clipped to y0 through y1, and the clipped length times the slice width is added.

`prect(px, py, x0, y0, x1, y1)` is the distance from the point to the rectangle (0 inside). `frect(px, py, x0, y0, x1, y1)` is the distance from the point to the farthest corner of the rectangle.

`area2(i, j)` is the area where the footprints of items i and j overlap seen from above. A box footprint is its rectangle from `lo` to `hi` in x and y, a cylinder is a disc of its outer radius, a ring is the disc of its outer radius minus the disc of its inner radius. Two boxes give the rectangle overlap area. A disc and a box give `cbox`. Two discs give `lens`. When item i is a ring, the result is the area of the other footprint shared with the outer disc minus the area shared with the inner disc (for two rings: the lens of both outer discs, minus the lens of i's outer disc with j's inner disc, minus the lens of i's inner disc with j's outer disc, plus the lens of both inner discs). When only j is a ring, swap the roles. Never below 0.

`gap2(i, j)` is the gap between the footprints seen from above, 0 when they overlap. Two boxes: the square root of the sum of the squared separations along x and y (each separation is the larger of 0, the low of one minus the high of the other, and the reverse). A disc and a box: `prect` of the disc center to the box minus the radius, at least 0. Two discs: the center distance minus both radii, at least 0. When item i is a ring (swap when only j is), with d the distance from the ring center to the other footprint: the outer gap is the gap of the other footprint to the ring's outer disc as above, the inner clearance is the inner radius minus how far the other footprint reaches from the ring center (for a disc its center distance plus its outer radius, for a box `frect` from the ring center), and the result is the largest of the outer gap, the inner clearance and 0; when both items are rings it is also at least the inner radius of j minus the sum of the center distance and the outer radius of i, so a ring sitting inside the other ring's hole keeps its true gap.

`pair(i, j)` measures two items. With zo the smaller top minus the larger base along z: `OV` becomes `area2`(i, j) times zo when zo is above 0, otherwise 0. With zs the larger of 0 and minus zo, `GP` becomes the square root of `gap2`(i, j) squared plus zs squared.

`need(i, j)` is the gap items i and j must keep. It is 0 when either item is the other's parent, when both kinds are among 1, 2 and 3 (plates and standoffs touch), when both are ducts, or when both are saucer frame parts (kinds 12 through 15) or one is a saucer frame part and the other is a motor. When one is a propeller and the other a duct, a saucer frame part or a motor of the same motor index, it is `dv`(50, 0.75). When both are propellers it is `dv`(2, 7). When either is a propeller it is `dv`(3, 5). When both have the same motor index of at least 0 it is 0. Otherwise it is `dv`(49, 1).

The exported function `dr_check()` returns an i32. It empties `CK` keeping its reserve. For every pair of items i below j that are not both saucer frame parts (kinds 12 through 15) it calls `pair`; with n equal to `need`(i, j): when `OV` is above 0.001 it appends i, j, 1, `OV`, `GP`, n, 0, 0 to `CK`; otherwise when `GP` is below n minus 0.000001 it appends i, j, 2, `OV`, `GP`, n, 0, 0. It stores the record count (the length of `CK` over 8) in `NC`, calls `dr_views` and returns `NC`.

`add(kind, shape, x, y, z, sx, sy, h, mass, parent, motor, axis, sign, group)` appends those fourteen values, then the current `NI` (its placement slot) and 0, to `IT`. When axis is above 0 and `NI` is below 64, the shift `MV` at index `NI` is added to the x, y or base z it just wrote (axis 1 x, 2 y, 3 z). It adds 1 to `NI` and returns the new item's index.

`body()` sets the body plate from the brief. With board `dv`(27, 36), cam `dv`(13, 19), g `dv`(49, 1), and the transmitter stacked on top of the flight stack for types 5 and 6 (for the other types it sits behind the stack): the half width is the largest of board over 2 plus 3, cam over 2 plus 3, `dv`(18, 12) over 2 plus 6.5 plus g and, when the transmitter is not stacked, `dv`(15, 25) over 2 plus 6.5 plus g (parts behind the stack sit between the standoffs, whose inner edges are 6.5 inside the body sides); `BP` 0 is minus the half width, 2 the half width, 3 (front) board over 2 plus 2 plus cam, and 1 (rear) minus (board over 2 plus 2 plus `dv`(19, 18), or plus the larger of `dv`(19, 18) and `dv`(16, 30) when the transmitter is not stacked). `BP` 4 is 4, 6 is `dv`(4, 5), 7 is `dv`(23, 2), and 5 (standoff height) is the largest of `dv`(44, 20), `dv`(14, 12) plus 2 times g, cam plus 2 times g and, when the transmitter is stacked, `dv`(14, 12) plus `dv`(17, 8) plus 3 times g. For design type 6 (toothpick) the front and rear edges are pulled in to 0.8 times their value, the half width becomes the larger of 0.8 times itself and half of `dv`(18, 12) plus 6.5 plus g, and the corner radius is 3.

`ang(k)` is the direction angle in degrees of motor k for the design type: type 0, 5 and 6 use 45, 135, 225, 315; types 1 and 7 use 90 minus s, 90 plus s, 270 minus s, 270 plus s with s equal to `dv`(43, 38) for type 1 and `dv`(43, 32) for type 7; type 2 uses 28, 152, 232, 308; type 3 uses 38, 142, 218, 322; type 4 uses 30 plus 60 times k; types 8 and 9 use 90.

`frame(m)` lays out the motors and arms at distance m from the center using the current `BP`. `NA` becomes 1 for type 8, 2 for type 9, 6 for type 4 and 4 otherwise. `BP` 10 becomes m. `AR` is resized to 12 times `NA` zeros. For each motor k with a the angle `ang`(k) in radians: for types 8 and 9 the motors sit at (0, 0); otherwise at m times the cosine and sine of a. Field 2 is a. The arm root is where the ray from the center toward the motor leaves the body rectangle (the smallest positive ray length that reaches the left or right edge along x or the rear or front edge along y), except for type 3 where the root is the body side edge at the motor's y (x equal to the right edge when the motor x is positive, the left edge otherwise). The arm length is the distance from the root to the motor, at least 0. Field 5 is 1.6 times the motor width and field 6 the motor width, where the motor width is `dv`(9, 11), or 5 for type 6. Fields 8 and 9: for type 5 the inner radius is the propeller radius plus `dv`(50, 0.75) and the outer radius that plus `dv`(21, 2.2); for types 8 and 9 the inner radius is `BP` 9 (the fan inner radius) and the outer radius the propeller radius; otherwise both 0. Field 10 is 1 for even k and minus 1 for odd k. For type 3, after the motors are placed, the body front edge grows to at least the largest motor y plus the motor width and the rear edge to at most minus that.

`holes()` fills `HO` with x, y, diameter triples for the bottom plate: the four flight stack holes at plus or minus half of `dv`(5, 30.5) in x and y with diameter 3.2 when that spacing is at least 25 and 2.2 otherwise; the four standoff holes 4 inside each body corner with diameter 3.2; and for every motor (except types 8 and 9) its shaft clearance hole of diameter 5.5 at the motor center and four screw holes at half of `dv`(6, 16) along both diagonals of a square turned by the motor's angle plus 45 degrees, with diameter 3.2 when that spacing is at least 16 and 2.2 otherwise.

`place()` empties `IT` keeping its reserve, sets `NI` to 0 and adds every part. With t `BP` 6, H `BP` 5, tp `BP` 7, R half of `dv`(1, 127), mh `dv`(8, 19), seat `dv`(25, 2), hub `dv`(26, 7), rho `dv`(35, 1.6) over 1000, and the prop base height pz equal to t plus mh plus seat (stored in `BP` 8). It then calls `place_frame` for design types below 8, `place_saucer` for type 8 and `place_ring` for type 9.

For every type except 8: the bottom plate (kind 1, box) centered on the body rectangle, base 0, height t, with mass rho times t times (the rectangle area plus, for every motor, its arm length times the average of its root and motor widths plus pi times the motor pad radius squared, the pad radius being the larger of half the motor width and half of `dv`(6, 16) plus 3.2); the top plate (kind 2, box) over the same rectangle at base t plus H, height tp, mass rho times tp times the rectangle area; four standoffs (kind 3, cylinder radius 2.5) 4 inside the body corners at base t, height H, mass 1.2, parent 0; the flight stack (kind 7, box, board by board) at (0, 0), base t, height `dv`(14, 12), mass `dv`(31, 20), parent 0, group 2; the camera (kind 8, box cam wide by 0.95 cam deep by cam tall) centered in x with its front 2 past the body front edge, base t plus half of (H minus cam), mass `dv`(34, 8), parent 0, axis 2, direction 1, group 2; the video transmitter (kind 9, box `dv`(15, 25) wide by `dv`(16, 30) long by `dv`(17, 8) tall) at x 0 with its rear 1 inside the rear edge, base t, mass `dv`(32, 20), parent 0, axis 2, direction minus 1, group 2, except for types 5 and 6, where it rides centered on top of the flight stack at (0, 0) with base t plus `dv`(14, 12) plus `dv`(49, 1), parent the flight stack and axis 0; the receiver (kind 10, box `dv`(18, 12) wide by `dv`(19, 18) long by `dv`(20, 4) tall) at x 0 with its rear 1 inside the rear edge, base t plus H minus its height, mass `dv`(33, 2), parent 1, axis 2, direction minus 1, group 2; the battery (kind 11, box `dv`(11, 35) wide by `dv`(10, 75) long by `dv`(12, 37) tall) at (0, 0) with base t plus H plus tp and parent 1, or with base minus its height and parent 0 when `DP` 46 is 1, mass `dv`(30, 180), axis 2, direction minus 1, group 3. Then for every motor k at its position: the motor (kind 4, cylinder radius half of `dv`(7, 28)) at base t, height mh, mass `dv`(28, 32), parent 0, motor index k, group 1; then its propeller (kind 5, cylinder radius R) at base pz, height hub, mass `dv`(29, 4.5), parent the motor just added, motor index k, group 1; then for type 5 its duct (kind 6, ring with the motor's duct radii) at base t, height the larger of `dv`(22, 0) and mh plus seat plus hub plus 3, mass 1.2 over 1000 times the ring area times that height, parent 0, motor index k, group 0.

For type 8 (saucer, an annular hubless fan driven at its rim around a ring bay and a hollow core) with w `dv`(21, 2.2), tg `dv`(50, 0.75), rm `dv`(8, 12) (the radial depth of the rim motor: rotor ring, air gap and stator), Hr `dv`(22, 82), rc `dv`(48, 0.445 times R), rw `BP` 11 and rf `BP` 9, every saucer frame part has mass rho times its footprint area times its height and group 0: the duct wall (kind 12, ring rw to rw plus w) at base 0, height Hr; the outer rim shell (kind 14, ring R plus tg plus rm to R plus tg plus rm plus w) at base 0, height Hr; when X equal to `dv`(52, 0) is above 0, the nose fairing (kind 14, ring R plus tg plus rm plus w to R plus tg plus rm plus w plus X) at base `dv`(53, 0.5) times Hr minus w, height 2 times w; the bottom skin (kind 15, ring rc to the rim shell's outer radius) at base 0, height w; the top skin (kind 15, the same ring) at base Hr minus w, height w; the spoke mount (kind 13, cylinder radius 22) at base half of Hr minus 12, height 4. Then the rim motor (kind 4, ring R plus tg to R plus tg plus rm) at base 0.45 times Hr, height 0.3 times Hr, mass `dv`(28, 750), parent the rim shell, motor index 0, group 1; the fan (kind 5, ring rf to R) at base 0.6 times Hr minus hub over 2, height hub, mass `dv`(29, 260), parent the rim motor, motor index 0, group 1; the flight stack (kind 7, box board by board, height `dv`(14, 12), mass `dv`(31, 20)) centered at (0, 0) on top of the spoke mount, parent the spoke mount, group 2; the transmitter (kind 9, its box) at (0, 0) on top of the stack, parent the stack, group 2; the receiver (kind 10, its box) at (0, 0) on top of the transmitter, parent the transmitter, group 2; the camera (kind 8, box cam cube, mass `dv`(34, 8)) at (0, 0) hanging right under the spoke mount, parent the spoke mount, group 2. The battery is c cells, c equal to `dv`(37, 4) times `dv`(45, 2): each cell is kind 11, cylinder radius 10.75, height 70.7, mass `dv`(30, 560) over c, evenly spaced on the circle of radius rw minus 10.75 minus `dv`(49, 1) with the first at half a step, at base w plus 0.5, parent the bottom skin, group 3.

`place_ring()` adds the parts of type 9, the twin ring: two stacked hubless rim-driven fans spinning opposite ways in one annular duct, a hollow sharp-edged elliptical chassis around the duct holding both rim stators and a ring controller board, and a camera pod in the middle held by arms that carry the battery cells: two arms when there are exactly two cells, four otherwise. Every part it adds has axis 0 and direction 1; its parent and motor index are minus 1 and its group is 0 unless stated. With R half of `dv`(1, 254), w `dv`(21, 0.8), tg `dv`(50, 0.75), rm `dv`(8, 7) (the radial depth of each rim stator), Hr `dv`(22, 40), hub `dv`(26, 10) (the axial depth of each fan), rho `dv`(35, 0.7) over 1000, rw `BP` 11, rf `BP` 9, rin R plus tg, rs rin plus w, rt rin plus 9 plus w plus `dv`(52, 14), zn `dv`(53, 0.5) times Hr, Li `dv`(57, 7) (the inlet lip radius of the chassis), li `dv`(58, 0) (the inlet lip radius of the inner duct wall, 0 for a plain wall), rg rw plus w minus li when li is above 0 and rw otherwise, zt Hr minus the larger of Li and li minus hub, zb zt minus `dv`(2, 4) minus hub, hv the larger of 1 and zb minus 2, cr half of `dv`(13, 28), ro cr plus 2.5, cl `dv`(10, 65.2), cw `dv`(11, 18.6), aw cw plus 1 plus 2 times w, za half of Hr minus half of aw, A rt minus rin minus 7, and c the whole number part of `dv`(37, 4) times `dv`(45, 1), at least 1, it adds in this order: the inner duct wall (kind 12, ring rw to rw plus w) at (0, 0), base 0, height Hr, mass rho times pi times ((rw plus w) squared minus rw squared) times (Hr minus li) plus rho times w times pi squared times li times (rw plus w minus 0.36 times li); the chassis (kind 14, ring rin to rs) at (0, 0), base 0, height Hr, mass rho times w times 2 pi times the average of rin and rt times (Hr minus Li minus 5 plus half of pi times (Li plus 5) plus 2 plus 1.07 times (the square root of (A squared plus (Hr minus zn) squared) plus the square root of (A squared plus zn squared))), remembering its index as ch; the chassis tip (kind 14, ring rt minus 3 to rt) at (0, 0), base zn minus w, height 2 times w, mass 0; then for each motor k from 0 to 1, with z equal to zb for k 0 and zt for k 1, the rim stator (kind 4, ring rs to rs plus rm) at (0, 0), base z, height hub, mass `dv`(28, 17), parent ch, motor index k, group 1, and right after it its fan (kind 5, ring rf to R) at (0, 0), base z, height hub, mass `dv`(29, 25), parent the stator just added, motor index k, group 1; the exit vane cascade (kind 15, ring rw plus w plus 0.4 to rin minus 0.4) at (0, 0), base 0, height hv, mass rho times 24 times 0.082 times (hv minus 0.8) squared times (rin minus rw minus w minus 0.8); the inlet guard (kind 15, ring rg minus 0.5 to rin plus 9) at (0, 0), base Hr, height 1.2, mass rho times (4 pi times (rg plus rin plus 8.5) plus 36 times (rin plus 7 minus rg)); the hub band (kind 13, ring cr plus 0.5 to ro) at (0, 0), base za, height aw, mass rho times pi times (ro squared minus (cr plus 0.5) squared) times aw, remembering its index as hb; na arms (kind 13, box), na being 2 when c is 2 and 4 otherwise, for k from 0 below na: with four arms, arm 0 points along plus x, 1 along plus y, 2 along minus x and 3 along minus y; with two arms, arm 0 points along plus x and arm 1 along minus x. Each arm is centered on its direction at the average of ro and rw from the center, rw minus ro long along that direction and aw wide across it (an arm along x is rw minus ro wide in x and aw deep in y, an arm along y the reverse), base za, height w, mass rho times w times pi times (aw minus w) times (rw minus ro), remembering the first arm's index as a0; the camera (kind 8, cylinder radius cr) at (0, 0), base minus 4, height Hr plus 8, mass `dv`(34, 30), parent hb, group 2; the controller board (kind 7, ring rs plus rm plus 1.5 to rs plus rm plus 1.5 plus `dv`(27, 10)) at (0, 0), base zn minus 0.8, height `dv`(14, 4), mass `dv`(31, 15) plus `dv`(32, 5) plus `dv`(33, 1.5), parent ch, group 2; then c cells (kind 11, box) for i from 0 below c, cell i on arm k equal to i modulo na at level j equal to i divided by na rounded down, cl long along that arm's direction and cw wide across it, centered on that direction at ro plus 1.5 plus half of cl from the center, base za plus w plus 0.5 plus j times (cw plus 1), height cw, mass `dv`(30, 188) over c, parent a0 plus k, group 3.

`clear()` returns an i32. Up to 48 rounds: for every pair of items i below j where neither is a propeller, a duct or a motor and they are not both saucer frame parts (kinds 12 through 15), it calls `pair`, and when `OV` is above 0.001 or `GP` is below `need`(i, j) minus 0.000001, it picks the mover, item j when its axis is above 0, else item i when its axis is above 0, else neither (skip the pair). With a the mover's axis minus 1, o the other item and n `need`(i, j): when the mover's direction is 1 the shift is `hi`(o, a) plus n minus `lo`(mover, a); otherwise it is `hi`(mover, a) minus `lo`(o, a) plus n; the shift is at least 0.25. The mover's position along that axis (center x for a 0, center y for 1, base z for 2) moves by the shift times its direction, and its slot's `MV` entry changes by the same amount. A round with no move ends the loop. It returns the number of moves in the last round.

`refit()` grows the frame around the parts after `clear`. For design types below 8: for every item of group 2 whose parent is 0 or 1, the body rectangle grows (left and rear may only go down, right and front only up) to hold its footprint plus 2 on every side, except a camera, which may stick out 2 past the front edge. For type 8: the fan inner radius `BP` 9 becomes the largest of itself, `dv`(47, 0.615 times the propeller radius) and `BP` 11 plus `dv`(21, 2.2) plus `dv`(50, 0.75). Type 9 keeps its frame as it is.

`pbad()` returns an i32: the number of item pairs, not both saucer frame parts, where at least one is a propeller, a duct or a motor and either `OV` is above 0.001 or `GP` is below `need` minus 0.000001, after `pair`.

`try(m)` calls `frame`(m), `holes`, `place` and `clear`, and returns `pbad`.

The exported function `dr_solve()` returns an i32. It zeros `MV`. R is half of `dv`(1, 127). It calls `body`; for type 8 it sets `BP` 11 to `dv`(48, 0.445 times R) plus `dv`(21, 2.2) plus 21.5 plus 2 times `dv`(49, 1), and `BP` 9 to the larger of `dv`(47, 0.615 times R) and `BP` 11 plus `dv`(21, 2.2) plus `dv`(50, 0.75); for type 9 it sets `BP` 9 to `dv`(47, 88) and `BP` 11 to `BP` 9 minus `dv`(21, 2.2) minus `dv`(50, 0.75). It runs three passes of `frame`(1.2 times R plus 30), `holes`, `place`, `clear` and `refit`, then zeros `MV` again. The search: when `DP` 51 is above 0, m is half of it; for types 8 and 9, m is 0; otherwise lo is R times 0.6 and hi is R times 4; when `try`(lo) is 0 m is lo, otherwise 36 bisection steps keep hi at a value whose `try` is 0 and m is hi. It runs the search, then `try`(m) and `refit`, then the search again, then `try`(m) and `dr_perf`, and returns `dr_check`.

The exported function `dr_perf()` returns an i32. With n the motor count `NA`, D the propeller diameter in meters (`dv`(1, 127) over 1000), rho air 1.225, V equal to `dv`(37, 4) times 3.7, eta 0.78, ct `dv`(39, 0.11), cp `dv`(40, 0.055), kv `dv`(36, 1900), duct gain g equal to `dv`(56, 0) when that is above 0, otherwise 1.15 for type 5, 1.25 for type 8, 1.03 for type 9 (the twin ring, from hover CFD) and 1 otherwise, and annulus factor af equal to 1 minus (`BP` 9 over the propeller radius) squared for types 8 and 9 and 1 otherwise: the frame mass is the sum of the masses of group 0 items; the all-up mass is the sum of all item masses plus `dv`(42, 25). The full speed in revolutions per second is kv times V times 0.75 over 60; the power at a speed s is af times cp times rho times s cubed times D to the fifth, and when the power at full speed over V times eta is above `dv`(41, 45), the full speed drops to the cube root of (`dv`(41, 45) times V times eta over (af times cp times rho times D to the fifth)). The thrust in newtons at a speed s is g times af times ct times rho times s squared times D to the fourth. Full thrust per motor in grams is the thrust at full speed times 1000 over 9.81. The hover thrust per motor in newtons is the all-up mass over 1000 times 9.81 over n; the hover speed is the square root of that over (g times af times ct times rho times D to the fourth); hover power is n times the power at the hover speed over eta; hover current is that over V; flight time is 0.8 times `dv`(38, 1500) over 1000 over the hover current times 60. `PF` gets 0 all-up mass, 1 frame mass, 2 n times full thrust, 3 that over the all-up mass, 4 hover speed over full speed, 5 hover current, 6 hover power, 7 flight time, 8 n times the full-speed current (the power at full speed over V times eta), 9 twice `BP` 10 for types below 8, for type 8 twice (the propeller radius plus `dv`(50, 0.75) plus `dv`(8, 12) plus `dv`(21, 2.2)) and for type 9 twice (the propeller radius plus `dv`(50, 0.75) plus 9 plus `dv`(21, 2.2) plus `dv`(52, 14)), 10 and 11 the overall x and y spans of all items (largest `hi` minus smallest `lo`), 12 the overall z span, 13 `NC`, 14 the all-up mass over (n times pi times af times (half of the propeller diameter in centimeters) squared), 16 the weight limit `dv`(55, 0) minus the all-up mass when that limit is above 0, otherwise 0. It calls `dr_views` and returns 0.

`lerp3(ne, th, c)` returns a real: the value in column c (1 drag area, 2 lift area) at tilt th degrees, read from the first ne rows of `AE` (3 values per row: tilt in degrees in rising order, drag area, lift area). Below the first row's tilt it is the first row's value, above the last row's tilt it is the last row's value, and otherwise it is the straight-line blend between the two rows whose tilts surround th.

`trim1(ne, w, u)` returns a real: the trim tilt in degrees at airspeed u meters per second for a craft weighing w newtons, or minus 1 when there is none. With q equal to 0.5 times 1.204 times u squared and top the tilt of row ne minus 1, it steps th from 0 up to top in steps of 0.05 and returns the first th where the tangent of th (converted to radians) times (w minus q times `lerp3`(ne, th, 2)) is at least q times `lerp3`(ne, th, 1). When no step qualifies it returns minus 1.

`tforce(ne, w, u, th)` stores the forces at airspeed u and tilt th in module reals: with q as in `trim1`, `FD` becomes q times `lerp3`(ne, th, 1), `FL` becomes q times `lerp3`(ne, th, 2), and `FT` becomes the square root of `FD` squared plus (w minus `FL`) squared.

The exported function `dr_trim(ne, w, tmax)` returns an i32; ne is an i32, w (weight in newtons) and tmax (largest total thrust in newtons) are reals. For every whole airspeed u from 1 to 40 meters per second: th is `trim1`(ne, w, u); it calls `tforce`(ne, w, u, th) when th is at least 0 and otherwise `tforce`(ne, w, u, the tilt of row ne minus 1); ok is 1 when th is at least 0 and `FT` is at most tmax, else 0. Row u minus 1 of `TR` (8 values per row) gets u, th, `FT`, `FT` over w, `FD`, `FL`, `FD` times u, ok. `PF` 15 becomes the largest u for which every row from the first up to and including it has ok equal to 1, or 0 when the first row does not. It returns 40.

The exported function `dr_ascore(ne, w, tmax, w0, u)` returns a real; ne is an i32 and the rest are reals. th is `trim1`(ne, w, u). When th is below 0 it calls `tforce`(ne, w, u, the tilt of row ne minus 1) and returns 10 plus `FD` over w0. Otherwise it calls `tforce`(ne, w, u, th) and returns (`FT` over w0) raised to the power 1.5, plus 10 when `FT` is above tmax.

The exported function `dr_hscore(p, p0, auw, limit)` returns a real; all four are reals: p is the hover power of a candidate, p0 the hover power of the starting design, auw the all-up mass in grams and limit the weight limit in grams (0 for none). It returns p over p0 when limit is at most 0 or auw is at most limit, otherwise 10 plus (auw minus limit) over limit plus p over p0.

`ofail()` records that the probe of parameter `OD` in direction `OS` did not help: when `OS` is 1 it becomes minus 1; otherwise `OS` becomes 1 and `OD` goes up by 1, and when `OD` reaches `ON` it goes back to 0, every parameter's step (`OX` at 5 times i plus 3) is halved when `OI` is 0, and `OI` becomes 0.

`odone()` returns an i32: 1 when `OK` has reached `OB` or every parameter's step (`OX` at 5 times i plus 3) is below its smallest step (`OX` at 5 times i plus 4), otherwise 0.

`oseen()` returns an i32: 1 when some row r below the smaller of `OK` and 64 has `OM` at 9 times r plus i within 1e-9 of `OC` at i for every parameter i below `ON`, otherwise 0.

The exported function `dr_opt_init(n, budget)` returns an i32; n and budget are i32s. `OX` already holds 5 values per parameter written by JavaScript: 0 best value so far, 1 lowest allowed, 2 highest allowed, 3 step, 4 smallest step. It sets `ON` to n, `OB` to budget, `OD` to 0, `OS` to 1, `OK` to 0, `OI` to 0, `OH` to 0 and `OF` to 1e30, and returns 0.

The exported function `dr_opt_ask()` returns an i32. It copies every parameter's best value (`OX` at 5 times i) into `OC` at i. When `OH` is 0 it returns 1 so the starting point is scored first. Otherwise it makes up to 8 times `ON` tries: when `odone`() is 1 it returns 0; v is the best value of parameter `OD` plus `OS` times its step, kept between its lowest and highest allowed values; `OC` at `OD` becomes v; when v differs from that best value by more than 1e-9 and `oseen`() is 0 it returns 1; otherwise `OC` at `OD` goes back to that best value and it calls `ofail`() (a candidate scored before is never asked for again). After the tries it returns 0.

The exported function `dr_opt_tell(f)` returns an i32; f is a real, the score of the candidate in `OC` (lower is better). When `OK` is below 64, row `OK` of `OM` gets `OC` at 0 through 7 and then f. `OK` goes up by 1. When `OH` is 0, `OH` becomes 1, `OF` becomes f and it returns 0. When f is below `OF` minus 1e-9, `OF` becomes f, every parameter's best value (`OX` at 5 times i) becomes `OC` at i, `OI` becomes 1, and it returns 1 (the next probe keeps the same parameter and direction). Otherwise it calls `ofail`() and returns 0.

The exported function `dr_opt_best()` returns a real: `OF`.

`main()` does nothing.